[2026-07-25 15:19:03] [CLIENT] [INFO] Answer:
MCP sampling works by inverting the normal request direction, where the server sends a CreateMessageRequest to the client, which executes the LLM call using its locally configured model and returns the result. This keeps all API keys and billing on the client side. In contrast, a regular tool call involves the client sending a request to the server, which then processes the request and returns a response. MCP sampling provides an additional layer of security and flexibility, as it allows the client to validate incoming sampling requests before forwarding them to the model, and it enables the server to craft prompts sent to the client's LLM. However, it also requires the client to implement a sampling_callback to handle the CreateMessageRequest and return a SamplingResult.
